<font class="storytitle" color="#FFFFFF"><b>Kabbalah Controversy.</b></font>
</td></tr></table></td></tr></table>

<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=6"><img src="images/topics/magazines.gif" border="0" Alt="Magazines" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><DIV class=verdana>A leader of <STRONG>London</STRONG>’s controversial <STRONG>Kabbalah centre</STRONG> – favoured by celebrities such as <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG> - has challenged the chief rabbi to defend his public criticism of the movement.<BR></DIV>
<P>
<DIV class=arial><STRONG>Dr Jonathan Sacks</STRONG> this week issued a warning about the <STRONG>US</STRONG>-based centre, which claims its version of the Jewish mystical tradition of <STRONG>kabbalah</STRONG> as a means of achieving spiritual enlightenment.<BR><BR>The centre, which has a £3.25m branch in central <STRONG>London</STRONG> funded by celebrity devotee <STRONG>Madonna</STRONG>, <STRONG>has been criticised for its money-raising and recruitment techniques</STRONG>.<BR><BR>A joint statement issued over the weekend by the chief rabbi’s office, the <STRONG>United Synagogue</STRONG> and the <STRONG>Beth Din</STRONG> said: “In the light of issues which have been brought to our attention relating to the <STRONG>Kabbalah centre</STRONG> in the <STRONG>UK</STRONG>, we wish it to be known that this organisation does not fall within the remit of the chief rabbinate or any other authority in the <STRONG>UK</STRONG> recognised by us.” <BR><BR>The statement has been backed by strong condemnation from members of the chief rabbi’s cabinet, including <STRONG>Rabbi Barry Marcus</STRONG>, who described the group as “<STRONG>a dangerous cult-like organisation that preys on vulnerable people</STRONG>”.<BR><BR><STRONG>Rabbi Yehuda Berg</STRONG>, son of the centre’s founder <STRONG>Rabbi Philip Berg</STRONG> and a spiritual advisor to the movement, told <STRONG>Totally Jewish</STRONG>: “We invite the chief rabbi to contact us to discuss those “issues” in his statement. <BR><BR>To date, we have never received any communication from the chief rabbi or any of his representatives. Perhaps, direct communication would be more fruitful than communication via newspaper articles.”<BR><BR>Describing the movement, whose followers include <STRONG>Britney Spears</STRONG>, <STRONG>Courtney Cox</STRONG> and <STRONG>Gwyneth Paltrow</STRONG>, as “a cult”, <STRONG>Rabbi Marcus</STRONG> of <STRONG>Central synagogue</STRONG> emphasised the importance of a unified communal stance on the centre. <BR><BR>He said: “They are operating outside the authority of the legitimate community. We feel a sense of shame and sadness they are using sacred Jewish learning to ensnare Jew and non-Jew alike.”<BR><BR>Fellow cabinet member <STRONG>Rabbi Yitzchak Schochet</STRONG>, minister of <STRONG>Mill Hill United synagogue</STRONG>, said: “If they are a non-profit organisation, why do they show exorbitant profits from the sale of their products on some of their tax returns? If they are genuinely concerned with people's souls, why are they known to prey on their wallets?”<BR><BR>But <STRONG>Berg</STRONG> defended the centre’s fundraising techniques: “<STRONG>All religions and spiritual organisations depend upon donations to defray the temporal cost of worship.</STRONG> The centre is no different. I do not think that the chief rabbi would dispute this reality. His own rabbinate is dependent upon such donations.”<BR><BR>He acknowledged there had been cases of “overzealous solicitation” and “overgenerous giving”, but said the centre regretted “any cases where it may have erred and apologises to any who may have suffered”.<BR><BR>Negative publicity will not prevent the centre continuing with its plans for expansion, and, according to <STRONG>Berg</STRONG>, <STRONG>the London branch will soon launch a “distant learning centre” manned by student-volunteers</STRONG>.</DIV><br><br><b>Note:</b> <i><STRONG>First published on a Madonna website.</STRONG> Source: <STRONG>Totally Jewish</STRONG>. </i>

</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


